# === ROLE ===
You are an expert variable extractor.
Task: extract structured variable–value pairs from clinical notes and supply verbatim evidence for each value.

# === INPUT DESCRIPTIONS ===
• <variable_list> – a table where each row gives  
   (the name of the variable)  (the type of the variable) (the description of the variable).  
• <patient_notes> – one or more free-text clinical notes.

# === INPUTS ===
<variable_list>
{{VARIABLE_LIST}}
</variable_list>

<patient_notes>
{{PATIENT_NOTES}}
</patient_notes>

# === EXTRACTION RULES ===
0. When extracting a variable’s value, rely on its description as the authoritative source, not the variable’s name. 
1. If a variable value is not present in the patient note itself, but can be directly, unambiguously, and confidently inferred from the patient note, use the inferred value as the variable value. However, DO NOT make diagnosis for the patient unless you are 100% sure. That is, don't infer disorders from symptoms or signs unless you are 100% sure.
2. If a variable is partially or completely missing value (which includes a lack of information on the implication - e.g., impacting viability), and it cannot be directly and confidently inferred from the patient note, set its corresponding entry to `"value": null`.
3. If the value of a Booleans variable is found, set it to either `true` or `false`.  
4. If the value of an Integer or Real variable is found, set its value to be the corresponding bare JSON number (no quotes).  
5. **Evidence** must be a single contiguous span  copied verbatim from the notes, and you should never distort the meaning of the original patient note during the evidence copying process (e.g., never extract 48-year-old as 8-year-old).
6. Produce a flat JSON object whose keys are **only** the variable names from the list—no extras, no comments, no markdown fencing.
7. You should always assume that the patient or other parties of authority (guardians, parents, etc.) is willing to comply or has complied with all logistics and has / has shown willingness to participate in any part of the study. However, if the variable concerns the person's demographics, personal relations, heritage, etc., don't asssume anything.
  7.EXAMPLE1: For parents' or guardians' consent, for exapmle "Patient and/or parents/guardian signed informed consent" should always be True. Similarly, "Patient and/or parents/guardian signed informed consent" is always true.
  7.EXAMPLE2: "Patients give their written informed consent for participation in the study and provide sufficient guarantees adherence to protocol according to the opinion of the investigator in charge of the patient care.", Always TRUE!!!!
8. In "IF_TRUE_SHOULD_BE_MENTIONED", decide on the following: if the variable is True, would a good patient note mention it? output YES/NO.
9. Ability to undergo procedure, if not explicitly contraindicated, should be set to True. 


# === OUTPUT FORMAT (output **exactly** these three blocks) ===

<patient_variable_values>
{
  "var_1": { 
    "description": "<when to set to true/value copy verbatim from the input>",
    "variable_mentioned_in_patient_note": "Yes" | "No",
    "variable_not_mentioned_in_patient_note_but_can_be_confidently_inferred": "Yes" | "No",
    "variable_not_mentioned_in_patient_note_but_can_not_be_confidently_inferred": "Yes" | "No",
    "variable_not_mentioned_in_patient_note_but_have_no_clue_to_be_inferred": "Yes" | "No",
    "value": "extracted value or null",        
    "evidence": "verbatim evidence for the value", 
    "reason": "<why you set the value>",
    "IF_TRUE_SHOULD_BE_MENTIONED": "Yes" | "No",
  },      
  // one entry per variable; order unimportant
}
</patient_variable_values>
